WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.807
Begin by framing Course Structure Overview as
a question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.000
observable results.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.375
The other thread is an independent project.

4
00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:18.521
Consider this worked case: A student might
choose 'Machine Learning in Healthcare' for

5
00:00:18.521 --> 00:00:24.667
the survey thread and 'Developing a Low-Cost
IoT Sensor Network' for the independent

6
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:25.140
thread.

7
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:32.199
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:32.199 --> 00:00:32.703
conclusion.

9
00:00:32.703 --> 00:00:38.546
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:38.666 --> 00:00:46.207
Now define Survey Paper Requirements precisely
enough that another learner could test the

11
00:00:46.207 --> 00:00:46.787
definition.

12
00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:53.720
Students will select, read and collectively
discuss papers from conferences and journals

13
00:00:53.720 --> 00:01:02.385
in CS. Consider this worked case: Students
will curate five recent ACM SIGCOMM papers on

14
00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:08.162
secure messaging protocols and synthesize
their methodologies into a comparative

15
00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:08.740
analysis.

16
00:01:08.741 --> 00:01:15.111
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:15.111 --> 00:01:17.428
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:17.428 --> 00:01:23.819
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:30.968
With the foundation in place, examine how
Independent Project Workflow operates in a

20
00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:32.051
concrete sequence.

21
00:01:32.051 --> 00:01:38.989
Students will select a topic, lay out weekly
goals, and meet with the instructor weekly.

22
00:01:38.989 --> 00:01:46.189
Consider this worked case: A student
developing a natural language processing tool

23
00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:52.789
will submit a one-page weekly milestone
document detailing progress, obstacles, and

24
00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:53.989
next steps.

25
00:01:53.989 --> 00:02:01.208
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

26
00:02:01.208 --> 00:02:06.848
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

27
00:02:06.962 --> 00:02:13.937
Next, compare the choices involved in Final
Deliverables and make the decision criteria

28
00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:14.474
explicit.

29
00:02:14.474 --> 00:02:18.849
A final report and presentation will be
required.

30
00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:26.388
Consider this worked case: The survey paper
culminates in a 15-page manuscript with an

31
00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:33.389
abstract, while the independent project ends
with a 10-page technical report and a

32
00:02:33.389 --> 00:02:35.005
10-minute defense presentation.

33
00:02:35.005 --> 00:02:42.371
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

34
00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:43.599
the goalposts.

35
00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:50.599
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

36
00:02:50.714 --> 00:02:57.617
Apply Admission Requirements to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

37
00:02:57.617 --> 00:02:58.679
isolated definition.

38
00:02:58.679 --> 00:03:04.554
Enrollment in this course requires permission
from the department offering the course.

39
00:03:04.554 --> 00:03:10.629
Consider this worked case: Students must
submit a proposal outlining their chosen

40
00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:17.717
research topics and a brief statement of how
the course aligns with their degree

41
00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:19.742
requirements before registration opens.

42
00:03:19.742 --> 00:03:27.081
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

43
00:03:27.081 --> 00:03:27.570
step.

44
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:32.382
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

45
00:03:32.498 --> 00:03:39.608
Examine the tradeoffs in Ethical Computing
Standards, including what improves and what

46
00:03:39.608 --> 00:03:41.385
becomes more difficult.

47
00:03:41.385 --> 00:03:43.573
They should respect privacy.

48
00:03:43.573 --> 00:03:50.979
Consider this worked case: When analyzing
datasets for the independent project, students

49
00:03:50.979 --> 00:03:57.768
must anonymize participant information and
explicitly state how data privacy was

50
00:03:57.768 --> 00:03:58.385
maintained.

51
00:03:58.385 --> 00:04:05.182
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

52
00:04:05.182 --> 00:04:12.447
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

53
00:04:12.554 --> 00:04:19.521
Stress-test the reasoning around Research
Integrity Practices with an edge case and

54
00:04:19.521 --> 00:04:23.004
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

55
00:04:23.004 --> 00:04:28.847
Responsible technical communication
distinguishes claims from evidence.

56
00:04:28.847 --> 00:04:35.207
Consider this worked case: Students will use
reference managers to track citations,

57
00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:42.098
ensuring every methodological step and data
source is properly attributed in the final

58
00:04:42.098 --> 00:04:42.628
report.

59
00:04:42.629 --> 00:04:49.160
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

60
00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:56.285
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

61
00:04:56.402 --> 00:05:04.350
Use Methodological Rigor to diagnose a failure
by separating symptoms, causes, and corrective

62
00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:04.961
action.

63
00:05:04.961 --> 00:05:11.711
Results should be reported accurately rather
than selected to create a misleading

64
00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:12.273
conclusion.

65
00:05:12.273 --> 00:05:19.529
Consider this worked case: When documenting
the independent project's algorithm, students

66
00:05:19.529 --> 00:05:26.785
will include pseudocode, parameter settings,
and baseline comparisons to enable independent

67
00:05:26.785 --> 00:05:27.445
verification.

68
00:05:27.445 --> 00:05:35.539
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

69
00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:37.273
smallest plausible cause.

70
00:05:37.273 --> 00:05:44.476
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

71
00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:52.417
Connect Stakeholder & Impact Analysis to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

72
00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:53.981
as one system.

73
00:05:53.981 --> 00:06:02.083
Ethical computing decisions identify
stakeholders, foreseeable benefits and harms,

74
00:06:02.083 --> 00:06:05.684
uncertainty, alternatives, and obligations.

75
00:06:05.684 --> 00:06:13.665
Consider this worked case: Students will draft
a brief stakeholder impact matrix for their

76
00:06:13.665 --> 00:06:20.505
independent project, mapping how their system
might affect users, developers, and regulatory

77
00:06:20.505 --> 00:06:21.075
bodies.

78
00:06:21.075 --> 00:06:30.423
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

79
00:06:30.423 --> 00:06:31.981
across representations.

80
00:06:31.981 --> 00:06:39.543
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

81
00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:46.195
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

82
00:06:46.195 --> 00:06:46.662
analysis.

83
00:06:46.662 --> 00:06:53.064
They should maintain high standards of
professional competence, conduct, and ethical

84
00:06:53.064 --> 00:06:53.646
practice.

85
00:06:53.646 --> 00:07:01.285
Consider this worked case: A learner maps the
inputs, constraints, and observable result for

86
00:07:01.285 --> 00:07:07.287
Practical Analysis Checklist, then checks each
step against the stated claim.

87
00:07:07.287 --> 00:07:15.304
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

88
00:07:15.304 --> 00:07:16.537
verification step.

89
00:07:16.537 --> 00:07:23.216
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

90
00:07:23.216 --> 00:07:23.693
intuition.
